WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
What did training change?. Compare attribution graphs of the same prompts before and after fine-tuning or RL, with a random null, a stability check and a validity check.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:13.240
The graph diff: what did RL change inside Qwen3-1.7B?

3
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:21.480
48 attribution graphs: 8 prompts, before and after RL, three training runs.

4
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:29.720
The same ten features the study picked by hand, in the same order.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:37.960
validity: ok -> RL did not make the base-model transcoders fit worse (FVU 0.500 vs 0.499).

6
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.060
The diff report: features gained, lost and reweighted by RL, against a swap null.

7
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.560
The top ten features, each checked against a control run that did not learn to hack.

8
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.560
Stability: two random halves of the prompts agree on 70% of the top ten (chance: 0.2%).

9
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.060
Validity: the transcoders were trained on the base model, so reconstruction error is checked.

10
00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.560
Same ten features as the study. 9 of 10 specific to the run that hacked, 6 replicated in another seed, reconstruction error unchanged by RL. Caveat built in: the transcoders were trained on the base model.
